  "text_sample": "Your Page Title Riflesso Menu Milan 00:00 Overview Buy Riflesso [email protected] Instagram Via Tortona, Milan Work Artists Films Archive Journal About Contact Shooting by the window Why Anders lit every room in Soft Rooms with nothing but daylight, and what that cost in time. Published 6 October 2026 Reading time 2 min All journal posts Soft Rooms was shot without a single lamp. Every room had to be photographed at the hour its windows were kind to it, which meant one room a day at most, and some days none at all. The brief came from a paint company in Copenhagen. They wanted their new colours shown in real homes, not on a set, and they wanted the walls to look exactly like the chips in the shop. Anders said yes before he had worked out what that meant. Waiting for the light The kitchen only worked at eleven in the morning, when the sun was high enough to stop hitting the floor. The living room worked at four, for about twenty minutes, before the building opposite took the light away. We spent the rest of each day moving chairs a few centimetres at a time and drinking a lot of coffee. Anders works slowly anyway. He sets up the camera on a tripod, frames the room, and then waits. H",
